ttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323049,"updateDate":1730147323049}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323049, 1730147323049),
    ($SATQ$aecdb820$SATQ$, $SATQ$29a4c0f5-e4a7-4ae9-93fc-4c153df627f7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Featuring works by the photographers Lola &Aacute;lvarez Bravo and Else &ldquo;Yva&rdquo; Neul&auml;nder-Simon, the <span data-ssml-say-as="date">2021</span> exhibition <em>The New Woman Behind the Camera </em>set out to provide a wide-ranging overview of photography by women in the 1920s through the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> given the collection&rsquo;s breadth of more than 120 photos, its efforts were largely successful.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"dc12ca83-582d-4665-bb5a-1f5798245277","content":"<p>1950s<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> and</p>"},{"id":"3ed57c81-4f76-4bf2-ae06-2a901edae9ff","content":"<p>1950s and</p>"},{"id":"a89abda9-e3d1-4cb9-9079-e852d0e74a3f","content":"<p>1950s</p>"},{"id":"fdaef0b0-de96-4ae4-b2b3-eb2d994319db","content":"<p>1950s<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["dc12ca83-582d-4665-bb5a-1f5798245277"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma and the coordinating conjunction "and" to join the first main clause ("Featuring...1950s") and the second main clause ("given...successful").</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because when coordinating two longer main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"aecdb820","external_id":"29a4c0f5-e4a7-4ae9-93fc-4c153df627f7","vaultid":null,"uId":"50e633d1-895a-44c3-8e97-3d5c15f42f29","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Featuring works by the photographers Lola &Aacute;lvarez Bravo and Else &ldquo;Yva&rdquo; Neul&auml;nder-Simon, the <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2021</span> exhibition <em>The New Woman Behind the Camera </em>set out to provide a wide-ranging overview of photography by women in the 1920s through the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> given the collection&rsquo;s breadth of more than 120 photos, its efforts were largely successful.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"dc12ca83-582d-4665-bb5a-1f5798245277","content":"<p>1950s<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> and</p>"},{"id":"3ed57c81-4f76-4bf2-ae06-2a901edae9ff","content":"<p>1950s and</p>"},{"id":"a89abda9-e3d1-4cb9-9079-e852d0e74a3f","content":"<p>1950s</p>"},{"id":"fdaef0b0-de96-4ae4-b2b3-eb2d994319db","content":"<p>1950s<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}],"keys":["dc12ca83-582d-4665-bb5a-1f5798245277"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma and the coordinating conjunction \"and\" to join the first main clause (\"Featuring...1950s\") and the second main clause (\"given...successful\").</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because when coordinating two longer main clauses such as these, it&rsquo;s conventional to use a comma before the coordinating conjunction. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061176,"updateDate":1755117061176}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061176, 1755117061176),
    ($SATQ$b0115ef6$SATQ$, $SATQ$06418ce9-cee6-42af-9e2e-f3c006985aff$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Technologies such as microphones and inkjet printers are made using piezoelectric materials, which generate an internal electric field when pressure is applied to them. The toxic nature of some of these materials recently led a team from the University of Sheffield to investigate how <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"24985b84-dcd5-42bf-8048-7c0a3f7c6cf5","content":"<p>could their use be better regulated<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"4ae568f5-757c-4469-9b1c-e176a25813b0","content":"<p>their use could be better regulated<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"},{"id":"8ffe7c2d-12aa-4f1c-a851-37103997e00a","content":"<p>their use could be better regulated<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"e8fcf2ac-b067-407c-8603-f8fe4fd9fbcb","content":"<p>could their use be better regulated<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4ae568f5-757c-4469-9b1c-e176a25813b0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is end-of-sentence punctuation. This choice correctly uses a period to punctuate a declarative sentence that asks an indirect question ("The toxic nature of some of these materials recently led a team from the University of Sheffield to investigate how their use could be better regulated").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the structure requires a period and a declarative clause at the end of the sentence that states what the team investigated, not an interrogative clause that asks a direct question, such as "how could their use be better regulated?" Choice C is incorrect because it&rsquo;s unconventional to use a question mark in this way to punctuate a declarative sentence. Choice D is incorrect because the structure requires a declarative clause at the end of the sentence that states what the team investigated, not an interrogative clause that asks a direct question, such as "how could their use be better regulated?"</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"b0115ef6","external_id":"06418ce9-cee6-42af-9e2e-f3c006985aff","vaultid":null,"uId":"2c16b06e-66e9-43cb-a5a2-d74620546e86","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Technologies such as microphones and inkjet printers are made using piezoelectric materials, which generate an internal electric field when pressure is applied to them. The toxic nature of some of these materials recently led a team from the University of Sheffield to investigate how <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"24985b84-dcd5-42bf-8048-7c0a3f7c6cf5","content":"<p>could their use be better regulated<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"4ae568f5-757c-4469-9b1c-e176a25813b0","content":"<p>their use could be better regulated<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"},{"id":"8ffe7c2d-12aa-4f1c-a851-37103997e00a","content":"<p>their use could be better regulated<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"e8fcf2ac-b067-407c-8603-f8fe4fd9fbcb","content":"<p>could their use be better regulated<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"}],"keys":["4ae568f5-757c-4469-9b1c-e176a25813b0"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is end-of-sentence punctuation. This choice correctly uses a period to punctuate a declarative sentence that asks an indirect question (\"The toxic nature of some of these materials recently led a team from the University of Sheffield to investigate how their use could be better regulated\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the structure requires a period and a declarative clause at the end of the sentence that states what the team investigated, not an interrogative clause that asks a direct question, such as \"how could their use be better regulated?\" Choice C is incorrect because it&rsquo;s unconventional to use a question mark in this way to punctuate a declarative sentence. Choice D is incorrect because the structure requires a declarative clause at the end of the sentence that states what the team investigated, not an interrogative clause that asks a direct question, such as \"how could their use be better regulated?\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323047,"updateDate":1730147323047}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323047, 1730147323047),
    ($SATQ$b0a525be$SATQ$, $SATQ$1dfa39cb-41ee-43a9-9afe-e30883ec6c25$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$57c556d3-b60c-425d-9357-0cc85eed6d58$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Santa Clara Pueblo artist Roxanne Swentzell&rsquo;s sculpture <em>Mud Woman Rolls On</em> consists of five human figures made of clay and plant fiber and arranged in descending size; each figure holds the smaller one in front of it. The arrangement of the figures, according to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> represents her idea that &ldquo;we all come from the Earth, generation after generation.&rdquo;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ba3d11dd-6f65-4271-97ad-7bfe4fb570db","content":"<p>Swentzell</p>"},{"id":"042eb0a5-ef29-40c8-90f1-91e869983838","content":"<p>Swentzell,</p>"},{"id":"bc327875-5353-4f0a-a314-6547b7afd03c","content":"<p>Swentzell:</p>"},{"id":"c954b721-6282-48c8-9283-f2dfa57c9fc6","content":"<p>Swentzell&mdash;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["042eb0a5-ef29-40c8-90f1-91e869983838"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The phrase &ldquo;according to Swentzell&rdquo; is an aside that interrupts the flow of the sentence, so it needs to be separated from the sentence with a pair of matching punctuation marks: two commas, two dashes, or a pair of parentheses. We already have a comma before &ldquo;according,&rdquo; so we must add a comma after &ldquo;Swentzell.&rdquo;&nbsp;. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a punctuation error. The phrase &ldquo;according to Swentzell&rdquo; is an aside that interrupts the flow of the sentence, so it needs to be separated from the sentence with a pair of matching punctuation 